# Security headers.
#
# Read by Cloudflare and applied to every response. Kept in the
# repository rather than clicked into a dashboard so that the rules are
# reviewable, diffable, and survive a project being recreated.
#
# The rule for this file: every origin listed must have a reason, and
# the reason is written below. Anything that cannot be justified is
# removed.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# What the policy allows, and why
#
# Everything the site is made of is served from this domain — one page
# of text, one photo, a stylesheet, two small scripts and six font
# files:
#
#   fonts    assets/fonts, instead of Google Fonts, so that no other
#            origin is needed for type
#   grain    assets/grain.svg, instead of a data: URI in the stylesheet,
#            so that img-src does not need data:
#   styles   css/style.css only; the markup carries no style="" at all
#   script   js/main.js and js/analytics.js; the page carries no
#            inline script
#
# The one outside service is Google Analytics, added at the business's
# request so that the SEO contractor can see where visitors come from.
# These are the origins Google's own CSP guide lists for a GA4 tag
# without Google Signals or Ads features, and nothing more:
#
#   script-src  https://*.googletagmanager.com   the gtag.js library
#   img-src     https://*.google-analytics.com    measurement pixels
#               https://*.googletagmanager.com
#   connect-src https://*.google-analytics.com    sending the measurements;
#               https://*.analytics.google.com    the wildcards cover the
#               https://*.googletagmanager.com    regional endpoints
#
# js/analytics.js loads the library itself; Google's inline setup lines
# live in that file, so no 'unsafe-inline' is needed. While its ID is a
# placeholder, nothing is loaded at all.
#
# If Google Signals or Ads features are ever switched on in Analytics,
# GA starts calling doubleclick and google.com as well, and those calls
# will be blocked here until lines are added — on purpose. Turn them on
# only together with a change to this file.
#
# The two <script type="application/ld+json"> blocks in index.html are
# not an exception. They are data for search engines, the browser never
# runs them, and script-src does not apply to them.
#
# Booking, WhatsApp, Instagram, TikTok and the map are plain links. A
# link leaving the site is a navigation, not a load, so none of those
# origins appears here — and nothing from them runs on this page.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Considered and not used
#
#   The Vagaro booking widget
#     Vagaro offers an embeddable widget. It would need script-src,
#     frame-src and connect-src for Vagaro and whatever Vagaro loads in
#     turn, for a feature a link already does. The link opens the same
#     booking page, in its own tab, with nobody else's code on ours.
#
#   Cloudflare Web Analytics
#     Cloudflare injects its beacon into the page when Web Analytics is
#     on for the domain. With Google Analytics in place a second counter
#     adds nothing, so it stays blocked here and should be switched off
#     in the Cloudflare dashboard.
#
#   Google Tag Manager
#     A container that can load any script chosen later in Google's
#     dashboard, which is exactly what this policy exists to prevent.
#     GA is loaded directly instead.
#
#   require-trusted-types-for 'script'
#     Would block Google's library, which builds script elements in
#     ways Trusted Types refuses. Our own scripts never write markup.
#
#   HSTS preload
#     A one-way door: browsers ship the list baked in, and removal takes
#     months. Worth adding once the domain has settled.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; script-src 'self' https://*.googletagmanager.com; style-src 'self'; img-src 'self' https://*.google-analytics.com https://*.googletagmanager.com; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://*.google-analytics.com https://*.analytics.google.com https://*.googletagmanager.com; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests

  # HTTPS only, for a year, including any subdomain.
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains

  # A file served as text/plain stays text/plain, whatever it contains.
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff

  # frame-ancestors above is the modern rule; this is for browsers that
  # never learned it. Nobody has a reason to show this page in a frame,
  # and a framed copy is how a fake "book here" overlay gets built.
  X-Frame-Options: DENY

  # The origin travels to other sites, the path never does. Vagaro and
  # Instagram still see that a visitor came from this site, which is
  # worth knowing; with one page, the path would say nothing anyway.
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin

  # Hardware and ambient APIs the site does not use and must not acquire
  # by accident. An empty list denies the page and everything it frames.
  Permissions-Policy: accelerometer=(), autoplay=(), camera=(), display-capture=(), encrypted-media=(), fullscreen=(), geolocation=(), gyroscope=(), idle-detection=(), local-fonts=(), magnetometer=(), microphone=(), midi=(), payment=(), publickey-credentials-get=(), screen-wake-lock=(), serial=(), usb=(), xr-spatial-tracking=()

  # Our own files cannot be pulled into another site's page. Link
  # previews are unaffected: WhatsApp, Facebook and Google fetch the
  # photo from their servers, where this header does not apply.
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin

  # Isolates this window from anything it opens. Every outside link
  # already carries rel="noopener"; this holds even if one forgets.
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin

# Font files never change without changing name, so they can be kept for
# a year. Everything else keeps Cloudflare's default, which checks for a
# newer copy on each visit — a new photo or price shows up at once.
/assets/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
